\textbf{Collapse is task-general.} To confirm that hidden-state routing collapse is not forecasting-specific, we run an AdaMix-style hidden-state router (mean-pooled $\mathbf{H} \to$ softmax gate) on the imputation task. Across 6 datasets $\times$ 3 seeds, \textbf{16/18 runs collapse} (entropy $< 0.3$; mean $0.143$ vs max $\log 5 = 1.61$), with the dominant expert (dense\_mlp) receiving $83$--$99.9\%$ of routing weight. AdaMix imputation MSE is $40$--$79\%$ worse than RR-MoA. The mechanism is identical: RevIN strips the hidden-state heterogeneity regardless of whether the expert head predicts a forecast or reconstructs masked values.

\section{Learnable Normalization Coefficient}
\label{app:learnable_alpha}

To test whether gradient descent independently discovers Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}'s prediction, we parameterize the router input as $\mathbf{r} = \sigma(\ell)\,\text{RevIN}(\mathbf{X}) + (1 - \sigma(\ell))\,\mathbf{X}_{\text{raw}}$, where $\ell$ is a scalar \texttt{nn.Parameter} initialized to $0$ (so $\alpha = \sigma(0) = 0.5$). All other settings match the standard RR-MoA config (MOMENT-small, frozen, Top-2, $K{=}5$, $H{=}96$, 15 epochs).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{\textbf{Learned normalization coefficient} ($\alpha$, 5 seeds). All 30 runs converge to $\alpha < 0.5$, confirming gradient descent prefers raw input for routing.}
\label{tab:learnable_alpha}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & Learned $\alpha$ (mean $\pm$ std) & MSE \\
\midrule
ETTh1       & $0.468 \pm 0.006$ & $0.463 \pm 0.015$ \\
ETTh2       & $0.482 \pm 0.007$ & $0.553 \pm 0.020$ \\
ETTm1       & $0.473 \pm 0.005$ & $0.397 \pm 0.012$ \\
ETTm2       & $0.466 \pm 0.003$ & $0.355 \pm 0.055$ \\
Electricity & $0.462 \pm 0.004$ & $0.269 \pm 0.008$ \\
Weather     & $0.459 \pm 0.009$ & $0.245 \pm 0.010$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

All 30 runs converge to $\alpha < 0.5$ (range $0.445$--$0.491$), with trajectories monotonically decreasing from the $0.492$ initialization. The unanimity is the key finding: gradient descent independently discovers that raw input carries more routing information, confirming the qualitative prediction of Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}. The narrow converged range ($0.459$--$0.482$) means the magnitude does not strongly differentiate datasets: the signal is binary (raw $>$ norm), not graded.

\section{Exact MI Loss and Bound Tightness}
\label{app:mi_tightness}

Since the router is deterministic ($E = \arg\max$), $I(X;E) = H(E)$ and the exact MI loss from Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}(i) reduces to $H(E \mid S)$. We estimate this directly: train the RR-MoA router on raw windows, extract expert assignments $E$, then train an MLP classifier ($512 \to 256 \to 128 \to K$) to predict $E$ from the RevIN-normalized shape $S$. The held-out cross-entropy (temperature-calibrated) gives $H(E \mid S)$.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{\textbf{Exact MI loss measurement and bound tightness} (3 seeds). $H(E \mid S)$ is the exact routing information lost to normalization. Retention $= I(S;E)/H(E)$. LB = lower bound from Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}(ii). Gap $= I(M,\Sigma; S \mid E)$ via within-expert CCA.}
\label{tab:mi_tightness}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccr@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & $H(E)$ & $H(E \mid S)$ & Ret\% & LB & Gap & $\Delta\%$ \\
\midrule
ETTh1       & 0.73 & 0.61 & 16.3 & 0.02 & 0.06 & $-43.2$ \\
ETTh2       & 0.76 & 0.57 & 26.3 & 0.00 & 0.10 & $-71.0$ \\
ETTm1       & 0.75 & 0.57 & 24.2 & 0.18 & 0.03 & $-51.1$ \\
ETTm2       & 0.82 & 0.53 & 35.1 & 0.18 & 0.07 & $-77.2$ \\
Weather     & 1.37 & 0.98 & 29.1 & 0.61 & 0.08 & $-44.6$ \\
Electricity & 0.68 & 0.46 & 32.4 & 0.13 & 0.02 & $-26.8$ \\
Traffic     & 0.63 & 0.44 & 24.6 & 0.19 & 0.02 & $+2.9$ \\
Exchange    & 0.63 & 0.44 & 22.1 & 0.00 & 0.10 & $-66.5$ \\
Solar       & 0.97 & 0.57 & 40.9 & 0.08 & 0.20 & $-32.9$ \\
