230:\textbf{MoE in Foundation Models.} Mixture-of-experts routing~\citep{fedus2022switch} has been used \emph{within} TSFM backbones to scale capacity, with experts placed inside the backbone architecture: Moirai-MoE~\citep{liu2025moiraimoe} routes frequency bands across internal experts, and Time-MoE~\citep{shi2024timemoe} uses sparse activation to reach billions of parameters. RR-MoA applies MoE on an orthogonal axis, at the \emph{adapter level} on top of a frozen backbone. The two axes are complementary rather than competing: stacking RR-MoA on Moirai-MoE further improves accuracy (\S\ref{sec:cross_backbone}, Table~\ref{tab:moirai_moe}).
438:\caption{\textbf{AdaMix routing collapse} (entropy max ${\approx}1.609$). Under unfreezing, entropy ${\to}0.000$. \colorbox{green!8}{Green}: disabling RevIN inside MOMENT recovers entropy ($0.66$--$1.32$), proving RevIN is the causal mechanism. Extended grid in Appendix~\ref{app:extended_rrmoa}.}
466:{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Why does standard MoE routing fail?} To understand the source of RR-MoA's advantage, we examine AdaMix~\citep{wang2022adamix}, which uses the standard recipe of routing on hidden states. Table~\ref{tab:adamix} shows that AdaMix collapses to a single expert whenever backbone layers are unfrozen: entropy reaches $0.000\pm0.000$ in 7/12 unfrozen configurations; the remaining 5 stay below $0.55$ (vs.\ max ${\approx}1.61$) with high cross-seed variance ($\sigma$ up to $0.71$), indicating stochastic collapse rather than stable partial routing. The green rows reveal the cause: disabling RevIN inside MOMENT recovers entropy from $0.000$ to $0.66$--$1.32$, proving that normalization is the causal mechanism (BatchNorm1d and GroupNorm produce the same collapse; Appendix~\ref{app:norm_generalization}). The causal contrast is sharpest when comparing backbones directly (Figure~\ref{fig:causal_contrast}): under identical last-4 unfreezing on identical data, MOMENT entropy crashes to $0.000$ in ${\sim}40$ steps while Timer-XL (which lacks RevIN) stays at $1.60$ for all $400$ steps, opposite trajectories from the same code.}
481:\caption{\textbf{Causal contrast: MOMENT vs Timer-XL} (ETTh1, last-4 unfreezing). Solid lines: unfrozen runs. MOMENT entropy collapses $0.51{\to}0.000$ in ${\sim}40$ steps (red); Timer-XL stays at ${\approx}1.60$ for all $400$ steps (orange). Dashed lines: frozen-backbone controls for both models, neither collapses. Dotted: $\log K{=}1.609$ ceiling. Same code, same data, only normalization differs. Pattern generalizes across all 3 tested datasets.}
548:{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Rawness vs.\ bypass and dose-response.} Replacing the router's raw input with RevIN-normalized input degrades MSE by $60$--$88\%$ while entropy stays high (Table~\ref{tab:router_input}, Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}), confirming the router relies on the \emph{content} of the stripped statistics, not merely the bypass path. A dose-response interpolation $\mathbf{x}_\alpha = (1{-}\alpha)\,\mathbf{x}_\text{raw} + \alpha\,\text{RevIN}(\mathbf{x}_\text{raw})$ reveals a \emph{phase transition} (Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}; 150 runs): MSE degrades by $+5.4\%$ for $\alpha{\leq}0.75$ but jumps to $+283\%$ at $\alpha{=}1.0$, while entropy stays in $[1.43, 1.57]$: normalization renders routing uninformative without reducing diversity. The phase transition threshold is quantitatively predicted at $\alpha^* = R(\mathcal{D})/(R(\mathcal{D})+1) \approx 0.40$ for the median benchmark (Appendix~\ref{app:prop2_depth}). A temporal-shuffle ablation confirms routing depends on $(M,\Sigma)$ statistics, not temporal ordering (Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}).}
566:In the limit where the router's signal lives entirely in the stripped statistics ($E \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid (M,\Sigma)$), the bound saturates and $I(X; E) - I(S; E) = H(E)$: RevIN destroys the entire routing distribution. We therefore use the \emph{signal ratio} $R(\mathcal{D}) = [\mathrm{Var}(M) + \mathrm{Var}(\Sigma)] / \overline{\mathrm{Var}(S)}$ as a tractable monotone proxy for the variance contribution of the stripped statistics. The bound is empirically tight: CCA gives $\varepsilon \in [0.09, 1.26]$ nats, cross-validated within ${\pm}0.26$ nats by KSG~\citep{kraskov2004ksg}; a held-out classifier shows normalization retains only $16$--$41\%$ of routing entropy with bound (ii) gap averaging $0.075$ nats (Appendix~\ref{app:mi_tightness}). Training $\alpha \in [0,1]$ as a learnable parameter, gradient descent converges to $\alpha < 0.5$ on all six datasets ($30/30$ runs; Appendix~\ref{app:learnable_alpha}). $R(\mathcal{D})$ predicts the raw-routing benefit at Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ ($p{<}0.002$, $n{=}9$; Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio}); $H(E \mid S)$ alone does not ($\rho{=}{-}0.12$), since routing \emph{potential} in the stripped statistics matters, not the MI loss itself. Traffic ($R{=}0.14$, $\Delta{=}{+}2.9\%$) is correctly predicted as a boundary case; Solar ($R{=}0.06$) is the one outlier (SSR ablation $+43\%$; Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}). Full proof in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}.
609:    xlabel={Signal ratio $R(\mathcal{D})$},
654:MoE adapters on instance-normalized TSFMs fail for structural reasons: the normalization needed for distribution shift also strips the per-window statistics a downstream router needs. Raw pre-normalization routing fixes the failure and motivates Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}; $R(\mathcal{D})$ identifies where it applies.
656:\paragraph{Implication.} Routing collapse on instance-normalized backbones illustrates a broader pattern: when a foundation model's input pipeline destroys signal a downstream router needs, optimization-side rescues (load balancing, z-loss, ReLU routing) cannot recover what is structurally absent: the fix is more readily approached by restoring the missing input than by retuning the optimizer. The same mechanism appears in a vision control (Appendix~\ref{app:vision_moe}); the signal-ratio predictor $R(\mathcal{D})$ predicts which datasets benefit from raw routing (Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $p{<}0.002$, with a held-out boundary case correctly flagged as low-benefit), and generalization to normalization families we did not test is bounded by the assumptions of Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}.
658:\paragraph{Limitations.} Three boundaries scope our claims. \emph{(i)~Normalization specificity.} Our diagnosis applies to backbones with instance-level normalization (RevIN, BatchNorm, GroupNorm); backbones without it (Chronos, Timer-XL) show no collapse and no RR-MoA gain, as Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} predicts. Extensions to attention-based or learned normalizers are not characterized. \emph{(ii)~Signal-ratio boundary cases.} The signal-ratio predictor $R(\mathcal{D})$ is a single monotone proxy for variance contribution; on Solar ($R{=}0.06$) it correctly flags low benefit but is not sharp. Datasets with strong shape-statistics coupling fall outside the tight regime of the bound. \emph{(iii)~Forecasting and imputation only.} We evaluate forecasting and imputation on LTSF benchmarks. Anomaly detection, classification, and irregularly-sampled tasks are deferred to future work.
1100:\emph{Tightness condition.} The bound~(ii) is informative when $I(M,\Sigma; E) \gg \varepsilon$. Since $I(M,\Sigma; E)$ increases with the location-scale variance, the signal ratio $R(\mathcal{D}) = [\mathrm{Var}(M) + \mathrm{Var}(\Sigma)] / \overline{\mathrm{Var}(S)}$ is a monotone proxy for the tightness of the bound. This explains the $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ correlation: high-$R$ datasets (Exchange, $R{=}2.17$) have $I(M,\Sigma; E) \gg \varepsilon$, producing large gains ($-68.5\%$); low-$R$ datasets (Traffic, $R{=}0.14$) have $I(M,\Sigma; E) \approx \varepsilon$, producing no gain ($+2.9\%$).
1110:\textbf{Rate-distortion interpretation of $R(\mathcal{D})$.}
1111:Instance normalization is a \emph{deterministic lossy compression}: it maps $X \mapsto S$, discarding $(M, \Sigma)$. In rate-distortion theory~\citep{cover2006elements}, this compression has rate $R_\text{strip} = I(X; M, \Sigma \mid S)$ and distortion (for routing) $D_\text{route} = H(E \mid S) - H(E \mid X) = I(M, \Sigma; E \mid S)$, which is exactly Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}(i). Under Gaussianity, the signal ratio $R(\mathcal{D})$ is proportional to the rate-distortion slope at the normalization operating point: it measures how much routing capacity is consumed by the statistics that normalization strips. Specifically, if the joint $(M, \Sigma, S)$ is multivariate Gaussian, then $I(M, \Sigma; E) \propto \log(1 + R(\mathcal{D}))$ (by the Gaussian mutual information formula), making $R(\mathcal{D})$ a \emph{monotone surrogate for the bits lost to normalization}. The validated $\rho = -0.88$ correlation (Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio}) is thus not a statistical coincidence but a consequence of this rate-distortion structure.
1115:Consider a softmax router on the interpolated input $\mathbf{x}_\alpha = (1{-}\alpha)\,\mathbf{x}_\mathrm{raw} + \alpha\,\mathrm{RevIN}(\mathbf{x}_\mathrm{raw})$. Under the Gaussian model with approximately orthogonal $(M,\Sigma)$ and $S$ (leakage $\varepsilon$ small), the effective routing signal power is:
1119:Routing discriminability crosses the router's noise floor at a critical normalization threshold:
1121:    \alpha^* \;\approx\; \frac{R(\mathcal{D})}{R(\mathcal{D}) + 1}
1123:For $\alpha < \alpha^*$, routing quality degrades continuously; for $\alpha \to 1$, routing collapses discontinuously due to the softmax's exponential sensitivity to signal-to-noise ratio.
1126:\emph{Validation.} For the median $R(\mathcal{D}) = 0.67$ across our benchmarks, Proposition~\ref{prop:phase_transition} predicts $\alpha^* \approx 0.40$. The dose-response experiment (Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}; 150 runs) confirms this: MSE degrades by $+5.4\%$ for $\alpha \leq 0.75$ but jumps to $+283\%$ at $\alpha = 1.0$. The sharpness of the transition arises from the softmax: when $P_\mathrm{route}(\alpha) / P_\mathrm{noise}$ drops below ${\sim}1$, routing weights converge to uniform regardless of expert relevance.
1182:The first term (self-gradient) always decreases $\ell_1$. The second (cross-gradient) is negligible when expert gradients are approximately orthogonal, a condition that holds empirically once experts specialize to distinct input clusters. The third (loss-coupling) term is small early in training when $|\ell_1 - \ell_2| \ll \|\partial\ell_1/\partial\mathbf{h}\|$. Retaining only the dominant first term:
1184:\dot{\ell}_1\big|_A \approx -\eta\,p\left\|\frac{\partial\ell_1}{\partial\mathbf{h}}\right\|^2\|\mathbf{x}\|^2
1186:and analogously $\dot{\ell}_2\big|_A \approx -\eta\,(1{-}p)\left\|\frac{\partial\ell_2}{\partial\mathbf{h}}\right\|^2\|\mathbf{x}\|^2$. When $p > 1{-}p$ (expert~1 dominates), $\ell_1$ decreases faster than $\ell_2$. The third term in $\partial\ell/\partial A$ further reinforces this: when $\ell_1 < \ell_2$, the factor $(\ell_1 - \ell_2) < 0$ drives $\dot{p} > 0$, pushing $p$ toward~1. Together these create the self-reinforcing loop: dominant expert gets more gradient $\to$ its loss drops faster $\to$ router increases its weight $\to$ it becomes more dominant.
1432:\caption{\textbf{Self-Routed MoA (SR-MoA): routing input control} (dense, 5 seeds unless noted). Each expert has its own sigmoid gate, with no external router. \textbf{Top}: gating on raw input vs hidden states (frozen, MOMENT-small). \textbf{Second}: Timer-XL negative control with no RevIN, so raw$\approx$hidden. \textbf{Third}: freeze-level ablation confirms Frozen Paradox (frozen best 4/6). \textbf{Bottom}: cross-backbone generalization (H=96, frozen). 252 runs total.}
1516:\textbf{Instance-adaptive gating (IA-Gating).} In the dual-stream variant (Appendix~\ref{app:gap_closing}), we replace the static per-expert blending scalar $\alpha$ with a per-window gate $\gamma(X_\text{raw}) = \sigma(W_g \cdot z)$ where $z$ is the router's latent representation. Across 6 datasets (frozen, 3 seeds), IA-Gating wins on 4/6 (ETTh2 $-8.0\%$, ETTm2 $-6.4\%$, ETTm1 $-1.9\%$, Electricity $-0.4\%$) and loses on 2/6 (Weather $+6.1\%$, ETTh1 $+0.3\%$). The learned gates cluster tightly around $\gamma \approx 0.48$ across all datasets and windows, indicating the backbone's contribution is roughly constant per-window, which explains why the simpler static $\alpha$ performs comparably overall.
1876:\textbf{Key findings.} (1)~Normalization retains only $16$--$41\%$ of routing entropy across all nine datasets, confirming that RevIN universally degrades routing signal. (2)~The bound gap $I(M,\Sigma; S \mid E)$ averages $0.075$ nats ($4.7\%$ of $\log K$), so the lower bound is empirically tight. (3)~Crucially, $H(E \mid S)$ does \emph{not} correlate with $\Delta\%$ ($\rho{=}{-}0.12$, $p{=}0.77$), while $R(\mathcal{D})$ does ($\rho{=}{-}0.88$). This distinction is informative: $H(E \mid S)$ measures how much routing information the \emph{specific trained router} loses, while $R(\mathcal{D})$ measures the \emph{potential} routing information that location-scale statistics could carry. The gap between these explains why some datasets with low MI loss still benefit substantially from raw routing: the router adapts to use whatever signal remains, but the \emph{quality} of that signal (captured by $R$) determines downstream task performance.
1887:\caption{\textbf{Backbone features vs.\ raw input} (H=96, 80/20 train/test split). k-NN: k-nearest neighbor regression ($k{=}20$, distance-weighted) on mean-pooled backbone features vs.\ raw 512-dim input. Ridge: regularized linear regression (cross-validated $\alpha$). Backbone features are $16$--$33\%$ worse than raw input even with a universal approximator (k-NN), confirming genuine information destruction.}
2064:\caption{\textbf{Closing the DLinear gap} (MOMENT-small, strictly frozen, Top-2, 3 seeds, H=96). Dual-stream experts that access both hidden states and raw input narrow the gap from $+33$--$75\%$ to $+9$--$11\%$ and \textbf{beat DLinear on Weather}. Learned $\alpha{\approx}0.49$ confirms the backbone contributes complementary information when combined with raw access.}
2081:\textbf{Result.} Dual-stream experts reduce the average DLinear gap from $+57\%$ to $+5\%$ (an $11\times$ reduction) and \emph{beat} DLinear on Weather ($0.198$ vs $0.208$, $p{=}0.03$, paired $t$-test). The learned blend parameters $\alpha_k \approx 0.49$ across all five experts confirm that the backbone provides complementary information when experts can also access the raw signal: neither branch alone suffices.
2104:\textbf{Why this matters for the diagnosis.} The fact that the gap closes precisely when experts recover the information our diagnosis identified as destroyed, and remains open when they cannot (FiLM re-injects only $\mu,\sigma$, yielding $+48\%$ on ETTh1 vs.\ $+9\%$ for dual-stream), is direct evidence that the diagnosis is both \emph{correct} and \emph{actionable}. The normalization--encoding bottleneck is not merely an observation; it is the causal mechanism whose resolution closes the gap. Importantly, the frozen backbone and raw-routed paradigm remain unchanged: these variants extend the \emph{expert architecture}, not the routing mechanism, confirming that the routing diagnosis (the core contribution of this paper) is orthogonal to expert design.
2106:\textbf{Expert pool ablation: canonical vs.\ alternative pool.} To test whether RR-MoA's benefits depend on the specific choice of 5 canonical pooling heads, we replace the canonical pool with 5 alternative expert motifs (multi-scale temporal blocks combining strided Conv1d, dilation, and residual gating) and rerun RR-MoA across all 6 forecasting datasets. Routing entropy is preserved under the alternative pool (no collapse under frozen backbone; entropy $>1.3$ across all configurations), confirming that the collapse-prevention mechanism is \emph{pool-independent}. Absolute MSEs (frozen, Top-2, 3 seeds): ETTh1 $0.764{\pm}0.015$, ETTh2 $0.667{\pm}0.069$, ETTm1 $0.583{\pm}0.075$, ETTm2 $0.844{\pm}0.241$, Weather $0.274{\pm}0.024$, Electricity $0.401{\pm}0.039$. The canonical pool is stronger by $8$--$24\%$ on the three datasets where we have both numbers, indicating that while RR-MoA generalizes across diverse pool designs, the canonical heads are better tuned for the forecasting benchmark: the routing architecture is the orthogonal contribution.
2247:    Blend gate $\sigma$ initialized at $b{=}{-}2$ ($\sigma{\approx}0.12$): model starts as effectively-DLinear; backbone added only where useful.};

